                    "details": "- `translate()` composes an additional translation with the current matrix; it does not restore it. The two-argument form adds zero Z translation, so from identity, `translate(0, 0, 7); translate(10, 20);` produces `(10, 20, 7)`. Use `popMatrix()` to restore a saved matrix or `resetMatrix()` to return to identity.\n- Units are the same logical pixels (or world units in 3D) as `drawRect` and the other drawing functions; see `drawRect` for the coordinate basics.\n- Order matters when combined with `rotate()` / `scale()`: `translate()` then `rotate()` rotates around the translated point; doing it the other way around rotates around the original origin and then moves along the rotated axes.\n- Usually wrapped in a `pushMatrix()` / `popMatrix()` pair so the offset does not leak into drawing that follows.",
